    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d366">

                <pb facs="0381" n="375" sameAs="frus:frus1942v02/pg375"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/24314: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers (Cole) to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-09-17T13:00:00+01:00">September 17,
                            1942—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 2:37 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>610. I learn from a well-informed military source here that high military circles
                    are very apprehensive that following the occupation of Madagascar<anchor n="54"
                        xml:id="d366-f1"/> the Germans may bring pressure on Vichy to admit them to
                    a share at least of the defense of the French Empire particularly North Africa
                    and Dakar because, the Germans are expected to say, you have proved yourself
                    incapable of defending it.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Vichy and Casablanca, true reading to Tunis.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cole</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d366-f1" n="54" xml:id="d366-f1-content">
                <p>Occupied by British forces beginning May 4, 1942; for correspondence, see <ref
                        target="#pg687">pp. 687</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
